WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.260
When a visitor types into the search box of an Orchard Core site

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.033
with a Lucene index, the text is read as a Lucene query.

00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:13.536
In this video, you see how it is parsed, and how operators, phrases,

00:00:13.536 --> 00:00:16.700
wildcards, fields and ranges sharpen a search.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.366
The search page hands the typed text to the Lucene search service.

00:00:25.366 --> 00:00:30.957
The service creates a MultiFieldQueryParser from the index's default fields and its analyzer,

00:00:30.957 --> 00:00:33.160
and parses the text into a query.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.155
Three things shape every search.

00:00:36.155 --> 00:00:41.828
A word with no field name is searched in the default fields, normally the full-text field.

00:00:41.828 --> 00:00:47.028
The standard analyzer lowercases terms and drops common English words, and a query

00:00:47.028 --> 00:00:49.510
that cannot be parsed finds nothing.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.798
Let's search for quince cider.

00:00:55.798 --> 00:01:00.491
With no operator between the words, a query finds documents that contain either

00:01:00.491 --> 00:01:02.810
word, so the page lists three posts.

00:01:03.281 --> 00:01:07.628
The operator AND, in capital letters, requires both words.

00:01:07.628 --> 00:01:12.111
Quince AND cider lists only the one post that mentions both.

00:01:12.573 --> 00:01:15.150
In lowercase, and is not an operator.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:19.526
The analyzer drops it as a common word, so quince and cider behaves

00:01:19.526 --> 00:01:22.823
like the first query and lists three posts again.

00:01:23.294 --> 00:01:28.203
A plus sign requires the word that follows it, and the other word stays optional.

00:01:28.203 --> 00:01:31.814
Plus quince cider lists the two posts that mention quince.

00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:35.559
NOT excludes documents that contain the next word.

00:01:35.559 --> 00:01:39.604
A minus sign or an exclamation mark does the same, but a hyphen

00:01:39.604 --> 00:01:41.773
inside a word excludes nothing.

00:01:41.773 --> 00:01:44.177
Quince NOT pruning leaves one post.

00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:51.773
Quotation marks search for words side by side, in that order.

00:01:51.773 --> 00:01:55.627
Fruit trees in quotation marks lists the two posts where the words are

00:01:55.627 --> 00:01:56.895
next to each other.

00:01:57.361 --> 00:02:01.223
A tilde and a number after a phrase allow up to that many

00:02:01.223 --> 00:02:03.062
words between its words.

00:02:03.062 --> 00:02:05.085
This is a proximity search.

00:02:05.085 --> 00:02:08.211
Pruning trees with tilde two lists two posts.

00:02:12.457 --> 00:02:15.518
A question mark stands for exactly one character.

00:02:15.518 --> 00:02:19.524
Replace the letter i of quince with a question mark, and the query

00:02:19.524 --> 00:02:21.917
lists both posts that contain quince.

00:02:22.389 --> 00:02:26.513
An asterisk stands for any number of characters, including none.

00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:30.165
The letters p r u n followed by an asterisk list the two

00:02:30.165 --> 00:02:32.109
posts that contain pruning.

00:02:32.591 --> 00:02:35.589
A wildcard at the start of a term is rejected.

00:02:35.589 --> 00:02:39.163
An asterisk followed by i n c e cannot be parsed, so the

00:02:39.163 --> 00:02:41.641
page shows There are no such results.

00:02:42.107 --> 00:02:45.100
A tilde after a single word is a fuzzy search.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.359
It finds words within two edits of the one typed, so the misspelling

00:02:49.359 --> 00:02:52.237
quinse lists both posts that contain quince.

00:02:52.755 --> 00:02:58.599
A pattern between forward slashes is a regular expression, and it must match a whole term.

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:02.435
This one matches terms that begin with p r and end with i

00:03:02.435 --> 00:03:05.235
n g, so pruning and pressing both count.

00:03:09.435 --> 00:03:12.266
Every indexed value has a field name.

00:03:12.266 --> 00:03:20.760
The display text is indexed as Content.ContentItem.DisplayText.Analyzed, and the full text as Content.ContentItem.FullText.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:24.909
A field of a content part is named after its part and its

00:03:24.909 --> 00:03:28.135
own name, once it is included in the index.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.043
A field name and a colon limit a term to that field.

00:03:33.043 --> 00:03:37.156
Here quince must appear in the display text, so the page lists the

00:03:37.156 --> 00:03:40.870
pruning post, not the one that mentions quince in its body.

00:03:41.329 --> 00:03:45.253
Only the term right after the colon belongs to the field.

00:03:45.253 --> 00:03:50.484
Parentheses put several terms under one field, and operators work inside them.

00:03:50.484 --> 00:03:54.719
Fruit AND trees lists the one post whose title has both words.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:59.647
Square brackets and the word TO make a range that includes both ends,

00:03:59.647 --> 00:04:02.142
and curly braces leave the ends out.

00:04:02.142 --> 00:04:06.480
The Normalized field holds the lowercase title, so p TO r lists the

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.560
three titles starting with p.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:17.653
Lucene queries accept the same syntax in a query_string query, such as one

00:04:17.653 --> 00:04:20.651
run from Search, Queries, Run Lucene Query.

00:04:20.651 --> 00:04:25.055
The text goes to a QueryParser, whose default field is the one the

00:04:25.055 --> 00:04:27.930
query names, or empty when it names none.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:34.159
There, a query that cannot be parsed shows an error that starts with Invalid query.

00:04:34.159 --> 00:04:38.900
And *:* matches every document in the index, whatever its content type.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:45.666
Four habits avoid most surprises:

00:04:45.666 --> 00:04:50.667
write AND, OR and NOT in capital letters, put a range inside brackets,

00:04:50.667 --> 00:04:55.800
put a space before a minus sign, and never start a term with a wildcard.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.737
That is how the search box reads a query:

00:05:00.737 --> 00:05:07.985
default fields, an analyzer, and Lucene's classic syntax for operators, phrases, wildcards, fields and ranges.

00:05:07.985 --> 00:05:09.610
Thanks for watching.

